Ovarian Cancer
Obstetrics Gynaecology & Womens health
Abdominal bloating/increased girth, Early satiety, Complex palpable adnexal mass

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OGW-026
Reason for Visit: Acute severe shortness of breath secondary to massive abdominal distension and intractable nausea.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and husband
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated acute onset of respiratory compromise (shortness of breath when lying flat) driven by rapidly increasing abdominal girth and vague pelvic pain over the last 4 weeks. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to assess for a massive malignant ascites or pleural effusion, and to coordinate immediate hospital transfer for diagnostic imaging and therapeutic paracentesis before the morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I cannot breathe if I lie down because my stomach has swollen up like a balloon over the last month, and I am too nauseous to eat a single bite.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms have progressed rapidly over the past month. The patient, aged 62, reports vague, non-specific abdominal bloating that has evolved into massive, increasing abdominal girth. She reports profound early satiety, loss of appetite, and difficulty eating, culminating tonight in intractable nausea and persistent indigestion. She notes unexplained weight loss (muscle wasting) despite weight gain specifically related to the ascites. She complains of new-onset constipation and significant pelvic and lower abdominal heaviness. Tonight, she developed orthopnea (inability to breathe while flat) due to the abdominal pressure.
Functional Status: Sitting strictly upright in a chair; severely impaired mobility due to abdominal weight and breathlessness.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of acute respiratory failure; husband is distressed.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/88 mmHg | HR: 105 bpm | Temp: 37.0 C | RR: 26 bpm | SpO2: 94% (Room Air) | Wt: 82 Kg (suspected large volume fluid)
General: Patient appears cachectic but with a massively distended abdomen. She is tachycardic, tachypneic, and using accessory muscles to breathe while sitting up.
Physical Exam: Respiratory exam reveals decreased breath sounds at the right lung base, suspicious for a sympathetic pleural effusion. Abdomen is massively distended, tense, with a positive fluid wave and shifting dullness, consistent with severe ascites. Deep palpation is difficult due to fluid, but a hard, irregular mass is suspected in the right lower quadrant. Bimanual pelvic examination reveals a palpable, solid, fixed, and irregular adnexal mass on the right, with distinct nodularity felt in the posterior vaginal fornix (pouch of Douglas).
Environmental Observations: Husband has set up multiple pillows to keep the patient upright.
Medication Reconciliation: Taking over-the-counter antacids with no relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Suspected Advanced Ovarian Cancer with massive malignant ascites - Status: Decompensating
Differentials:

Congestive Heart Failure - Unlikely given the specific presence of a fixed, hard pelvic mass and cul-de-sac nodularity, though right-sided failure can cause ascites.

Liver Cirrhosis - A differential for massive ascites, but the palpable complex adnexal mass points strongly to a gynecological primary malignancy.

Advanced Colorectal Cancer - Also possible and can present similarly with ascites and pelvic masses, but the specific constellation of early satiety and fixed adnexal mass heavily favors ovarian etiology.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High (Acute respiratory compromise)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with the classic, though often late, signs of advanced ovarian malignancy: massive ascites causing secondary respiratory distress (orthopnea), early satiety, and a palpable, fixed, irregular pelvic mass with cul-de-sac nodularity. The acute complication of respiratory compromise due to the physical splinting of the diaphragm by the massive ascites required urgent after-hours evaluation. Delayed assessment until the next in-hours period would have risked progressive respiratory failure and unmanageable distress. The immediate clinical requirement is emergency ambulance transfer to a tertiary hospital for acute therapeutic paracentesis (to relieve breathing) and urgent staging imaging (CT/ultrasound).

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered sublingual ondansetron 4mg for intractable nausea. Kept the patient in a strictly upright, seated position to maximize diaphragmatic excursion. Activated Emergency Medical Services (000) for immediate transport to the Emergency Department for acute respiratory stabilization, therapeutic paracentesis (fluid drainage), and urgent oncology/gynecology referral.
Positive Behavioral Change: To maximize your ability to breathe right now, lean slightly forward and rest your arms on a table or your knees (the "tripod" position); feeling the immediate decrease in breathing effort will reinforce this posture while we wait for the ambulance. To assist the hospital team, clearly state that you cannot lie flat under any circumstances; advocating for this physical limit will prevent acute breathing crises during your transport and scans.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Emergency Medical Services (Ambulance) activated and on route.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not applicable.
Follow-up: Direct clinical handover provided to the responding paramedic crew.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Ambulance dispatched for immediate emergency transport to the hospital due to acute respiratory compromise and need for urgent fluid drainage.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The clinical signs strongly suggest a significant abdominal malignancy causing fluid buildup; definitive diagnosis requires hospital-based scans and fluid analysis.
Clinical Course: The ambulance will take you to the hospital where they can safely drain the fluid from your stomach using a small tube. This will immediately improve your breathing and nausea.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not attempt to lie flat, walk, or eat anything while waiting for the paramedics.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: There appears to be a large growth in your pelvis that is irritating the lining of your abdomen, causing it to produce a massive amount of fluid. This fluid is pressing up against your lungs, which is why you cannot breathe when you lie down. You need to go to the hospital immediately so they can drain this fluid safely to help you breathe, and then they will perform detailed scans to determine exactly what the mass is.
Actionable Steps: Remain sitting upright. Take slow, deep breaths. Do not eat or drink anything, as you will likely need scans or procedures soon after arriving at the hospital.

